      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `vllm/vllm-openai:v0.33.0` image ships an `lmcache` wheel built for torch 2.13 on torch 2.14. `lmcache.cuda_ops` fails to load, so KV offloading falls back or fails. This rebuilds `lmcache` against the installed torch, only when the import fails.\n\nFixes #53424. Supersedes #53441 (thanks @mehrdadbn9), which is stale against the current Dockerfile.\n\n## Claims\n\n- `import torch, lmcache.cuda_ops` succeeds in the built image.\n- No rebuild when the wheel already matches torch.\n- The build fails if the rebuilt extension still does not load.\n\n## Validation\n\nPublished image (amd64 `sha256:91ee972ab751fb7180bc71c175865e36b091686600dca783d52b2511399810b3`):\n\n```console\n$ docker run --rm --entrypoint python3 vllm/vllm-openai:v0.33.0 -c \"import torch, lmcache.cuda_ops\"\nImportError: .../lmcache/cuda_ops.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so: undefined symbol: _ZN3c104cuda29c10_cuda_check_implementationEiPKcS2_jb\n```\n\nThe new Dockerfile block, run on that image on a native amd64 node (16 CPU, `CUDA_MAJOR=13`):\n\n```console\n      Built lmcache==0.5.6\nPrepared 1 package in 8m 09s\n ~ lmcache==0.5.6\n$ python3 -c \"import torch, lmcache.cuda_ops, lmcache.lmcache_fs, lmcache.lmcache_native, lmcache.lmcache_redis; print(torch.__version__)\"\n2.14.0+cu130\n```\n\n- `pip freeze` differs only by the added `grpcio-tools==1.78.0`.\n- A wheel built the same way has served GLM-5.3-Flash on H100 and B200, with LMCache host-cache store and reload checked.\n- `pre-commit` and `shellcheck` pass on both files.\n- Not run: a full `docker build`.\n\n## Details\n\n**Root cause:** the `lmcache` sdist pins `torch==2.13.0` for its build, and its wheels link private c10 symbols, so they load only on that torch. The existing `--no-build` fallback never runs: the wheel installs fine and fails only at import.\n\n**Fix:** if the import fails, install the CUDA sparse/solver headers and `grpcio-tools` (matching `grpcio`), rebuild the same `lmcache` version with `--no-build-isolation`, recheck the import, then purge the headers. `LMCACHE_CUDA_MAJOR` follows `CUDA_MAJOR`. `.buildkite/scripts/install-kv-connectors.sh` gets the same logic.\n\n**Why not #53441:** it always builds from source, hardcodes `lmcache >= 0.3.9`, has no import check, and targets an older Dockerfile.\n\n**Trade-offs:** about 8 minutes on 16 CPUs, only on mismatched builds; `grpcio-tools` stays installed. The rebuild stops on its own once LMCache ships wheels for vLLM's torch.\n\nAI assistance (Claude) was used to investigate, write and validate this change. I reviewed every changed line and the validation output.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
